Depending on the interface (`ITodoStore`) lets you swap in EF Core or a test double without changing endpoints. Singletons with mutable state need thread safety in real use.

Sealed types restrict subclasses, so the compiler can check that a `switch` is exhaustive.

```java
public class Shapes {
    sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Triangle {}
    record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {}
    record Square(double side) implements Shape {}
    record Triangle(double base, double height) implements Shape {}

    static double area(Shape s) {
        return switch (s) {
            case Circle c when c.radius() < 0 -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative radius");
            case Circle c   -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
            case Square sq  -> sq.side() * sq.side();
            case Triangle t -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
            // no default needed: the sealed hierarchy is exhaustive
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(area(new Circle(1)));
        System.out.println(area(new Triangle(4, 3)));   // 6.0
    }
}
```

Adding a new permitted subtype breaks compilation of every exhaustive `switch`, which is exactly what you want when evolving a domain model.

Virtual threads are lightweight and cheap, so you can create one per blocking task.

```java
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;

public class Fetcher {
    private static final HttpClient CLIENT = HttpClient.newHttpClient();

    static String fetch(String url) throws Exception {
        var req = HttpRequest.newBuilder(URI.create(url)).build();
        return CLIENT.send(req, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString()).body();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<String> urls = List.of(
            "https://example.com", "https://example.org", "https://example.net");

        try (ExecutorService exec = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            List<Future<String>> futures = urls.stream()
                .map(u -> exec.submit(() -> fetch(u)))
                .toList();
            for (var f : futures)
                System.out.println(f.get().length() + " chars");
        }   // try-with-resources waits for all tasks
    }
}
```

Don't pool virtual threads, and avoid `synchronized` blocks around long I/O because they can pin the carrier thread. Prefer `ReentrantLock` in that case.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class Sales {
    record Sale(String region, String product, int units, double price) {
        double revenue() { return units * price; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Sale> sales = List.of(
            new Sale("EU", "Laptop", 3, 1200),
            new Sale("EU", "Mouse", 10, 25),
            new Sale("US", "Laptop", 5, 1100),
            new Sale("US", "Mouse", 2, 30));

        // Total revenue per region, sorted
        Map<String, Double> byRegion = sales.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Sale::region,
                     TreeMap::new,
                     Collectors.summingDouble(Sale::revenue)));
        System.out.println(byRegion);

        // Best-selling product per region (by units)
        Map<String, Optional<Sale>> best = sales.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Sale::region,
                     Collectors.maxBy(Comparator.comparingInt(Sale::units))));

        // Partition into big and small orders
        Map<Boolean, List<String>> big = sales.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.partitioningBy(s -> s.units() >= 5,
                     Collectors.mapping(Sale::product, Collectors.toList())));
        System.out.println(big);
    }
}
```

`groupingBy` accepts a map factory and a downstream collector, which lets you compose aggregations in a single pass.

```java
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LruCache<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final Map<K, V> map;

    public LruCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        // accessOrder = true makes iteration order least-recently-used first
        this.map = new LinkedHashMap<>(capacity, 0.75f, true) {
            @Override
            protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
                return size() > LruCache.this.capacity;
            }
        };
    }

    public synchronized V get(K key) { return map.get(key); }
    public synchronized void put(K key, V value) { map.put(key, value); }
    public synchronized int size() { return map.size(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LruCache<String, Integer> c = new LruCache<>(2);
        c.put("a", 1); c.put("b", 2);
        c.get("a");          // a is now most recent
        c.put("c", 3);       // evicts b
        System.out.println(c.get("b"));   // null
    }
}
```

The `synchronized` methods make it safe for concurrent use. For high contention, consider Caffeine, which is what most Java services use in practice.

Knuth-Morris-Pratt finds a pattern in O(n + m) by never re-scanning text characters.

```java
public class KMP {
    static int[] buildLps(String p) {
        int[] lps = new int[p.length()];
        for (int i = 1, len = 0; i < p.length(); ) {
            if (p.charAt(i) == p.charAt(len)) {
                lps[i++] = ++len;
            } else if (len > 0) {
                len = lps[len - 1];           // fall back, don't advance i
            } else {
                lps[i++] = 0;
            }
        }
        return lps;
    }

    static java.util.List<Integer> search(String text, String pat) {
        var result = new java.util.ArrayList<Integer>();
        if (pat.isEmpty()) return result;
        int[] lps = buildLps(pat);
        for (int i = 0, j = 0; i < text.length(); ) {
            if (text.charAt(i) == pat.charAt(j)) {
                i++; j++;
                if (j == pat.length()) {
                    result.add(i - j);
                    j = lps[j - 1];
                }
            } else if (j > 0) {
                j = lps[j - 1];
            } else {
                i++;
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(search("abababcabab", "abab"));   // [0, 2, 7]
    }
}
```

`lps[i]` stores the length of the longest proper prefix of the pattern that is also a suffix of `pat[0..i]`.

A Proxy intercepts property reads and writes, letting you build simple reactivity (the idea behind Vue 3).

```javascript
function reactive(target, onChange) {
  return new Proxy(target, {
    get(obj, key, receiver) {
      const value = Reflect.get(obj, key, receiver);
      return typeof value === "object" && value !== null
        ? reactive(value, onChange)          // deep reactivity, lazily wrapped
        : value;
    },
    set(obj, key, value, receiver) {
      const ok = Reflect.set(obj, key, value, receiver);
      onChange(key, value);
      return ok;
    },
    deleteProperty(obj, key) {
      const ok = Reflect.deleteProperty(obj, key);
      onChange(key, undefined);
      return ok;
    },
  });
}

const state = reactive({ user: { name: "Ana" }, items: [] }, (k, v) => {
  console.log(`changed ${String(k)} ->`, v);
});

state.user.name = "Omar";   // logs: changed name -> Omar
state.items.push(1);        // logs: changed 0 -> 1, changed length -> 1
```

Note that `Reflect` is used instead of `obj[key]` so getters and setters receive the correct `this`.

Async generators let you compose streams of data lazily with backpressure.

```javascript
async function* readLines(lines) {
  for (const line of lines) {
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 50));   // simulate I/O
    yield line;
  }
}

async function* map(source, fn) {
  for await (const x of source) yield fn(x);
}

async function* filter(source, pred) {
  for await (const x of source) if (pred(x)) yield x;
}

async function* take(source, n) {
  if (n <= 0) return;
  for await (const x of source) {
    yield x;
    if (--n === 0) return;     // stops pulling from upstream
  }
}

(async () => {
  const lines = ["alpha", "beta", "", "gamma", "delta"];
  const pipeline = take(
    map(filter(readLines(lines), s => s.length > 0), s => s.toUpperCase()),
    3
  );
  for await (const out of pipeline) console.log(out);  // ALPHA, BETA, GAMMA
})();
```

Nothing runs until the consumer pulls. When `take` returns, `for await` calls `.return()` on the upstream generators, so resources are cleaned up.

Web Workers run JavaScript on a background thread so the UI stays responsive.

`worker.js`:
```javascript
self.onmessage = ({ data }) => {
  const { start, end } = data;
  let count = 0;
  for (let n = start; n < end; n++) {
    let prime = n > 1;
    for (let d = 2; d * d <= n && prime; d++) if (n % d === 0) prime = false;
    if (prime) count++;
  }
  self.postMessage({ count });
};
```

`main.js`:
```javascript
const worker = new Worker("worker.js");
worker.onmessage = ({ data }) => {
  document.querySelector("#result").textContent = `Primes: ${data.count}`;
};
worker.onerror = e => console.error("Worker failed:", e.message);

document.querySelector("#run").addEventListener("click", () => {
  worker.postMessage({ start: 0, end: 5_000_000 });  // UI stays responsive
});

// later: worker.terminate();
```

Workers can't touch the DOM. Messages are copied with the structured clone algorithm; pass large `ArrayBuffer`s as transferables (`postMessage(buf, [buf])`) to avoid copies.

```html
<img data-src="photo1.jpg" alt="Photo 1" width="600" height="400">
<img data-src="photo2.jpg" alt="Photo 2" width="600" height="400">

